From: "Barak A. Pearlmutter" <barak+git@pearlmutter.net>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:11:22 +0100
Subject: option to compare files not named *.pdf

Files to compare had to have names ending in .pdf: on the command line
other names were "unrecognized arguments", and the file dialogs showed
only *.pdf.  PDFs are not always named that way (e.g., downloads, or
files from version control tools), so:

- --any-extension accepts any file names on the command line, in batch
  mode and the GUI;
- the Options dialog's General tab has "Files to compare" radio buttons,
  "Only files named *.pdf" (the default) and "Any file name", saved as
  the RequirePdfExtension setting; with "Any file name" the GUI's file
  dialogs show all files, with *.pdf still offered as a filter, and the
  GUI accepts any names on its command line.  Batch mode ignores this
  setting, so its results do not depend on GUI preferences.

main() now collects the arguments that are not options and chooses the
files after parsing them all, since -b may follow the file names.
Unknown arguments starting with "-" are still errors (testbase.sh's
-sgurble), and "--" still ends the options.

Tests check that a .dat copy of a test PDF is refused without
--any-extension and compared with it.  The help lists --any-extension.

